<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs">
  <Package Name="KanaAI Development Preview" Manufacturer="KanaAI Project" Version="$(var.Version)"
           UpgradeCode="381B4CC9-ABAA-4AB2-9DC8-FCA54CE3B964" Language="1041" Scope="perMachine" InstallerVersion="500">
    <MajorUpgrade Schedule="afterInstallInitialize" DowngradeErrorMessage="新しいバージョンのKanaAIがインストールされています。" />
    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" CompressionLevel="high" />
    <Launch Condition="VersionNT64" Message="64bit版Windowsが必要です。" />
    <Property Id="ARPNOMODIFY" Value="1" />
    <Property Id="ARPURLINFOABOUT" Value="https://github.com/aruiki/kanai" />
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
      <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="KanaAI" />
    </StandardDirectory>
    <Feature Id="Core" Title="KanaAI" Level="1">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="RuntimeFiles" />
      <ComponentRef Id="EnableKanaAiForCurrentUser" />
    </Feature>
    <Binary Id="Registrar" SourceFile="$(var.HelperPath)" />
    <SetProperty Id="RegisterTIP" Value="[INSTALLFOLDER]" Before="RegisterTIP" Sequence="execute" />
    <SetProperty Id="UnregisterRollback" Value="[INSTALLFOLDER]" Before="UnregisterRollback" Sequence="execute" />
    <!--
      EnableTipProfile is handed the install root, the way RegisterTIP already
      was; it previously had no SetProperty and so ran with empty CustomActionData.

      Why this line is here, stated honestly: it is a consistency fix, **not** a
      fix for a measured failure. Adding it, rebuilding, and reinstalling moved
      nothing: the per-user `TIP\...\LanguageProfile\0x00000411\...\Enable`
      record that Microsoft IME has is still absent for KanaAI, and
      `EnableTipProfile` still returns success without writing it to the
      installing user's hive. The per-user record is written by the HKCU
      component below instead, for the reason given there.

      A correction to what this comment used to say
      =============================================
      An earlier revision of this comment recorded an activation defect as
      measured fact: that `IClassFactory::CreateInstance` faults with
      0xC0000005 in `mozc_tip64.dll`, that the DLL is loaded in zero
      processes, and that this is a defect in the TIP build.

      **That was wrong, and it was wrong because the probe was wrong.**
      Re-measured on 2026-09-28 against the installed DLL (SHA-256
      5BE0B94FBCD0816771E76509AEBCC2CE3C9533BE7AD84F1E5632F0F9CFDFA47D),
      loading the DLL by absolute path and asking that DLL for its own class
      object:

        DllGetClassObject                      S_OK
        IClassFactory::CreateInstance          S_OK   (with a real IID_IUnknown)
        instance pointer                        non-null
        IUnknown::QueryInterface               S_OK
        reference count after QI               2      (CreateInstance made one)
        verdict: the class factory returned a live IUnknown; the TIP is
                 constructible

      The 0xC0000005 was an artefact of the probe's own declaration. It
      declared

        HRESULT CreateInstance(IntPtr rclsid, IntPtr pUnkOuter, out object ppv)

      and called it with two nulls. The first parameter is `pUnkOuter`, not an
      IID, so `NULL` was passed as the `riid` and the TIP's own `IsIIDOf` did
      `memcmp(NULL, &IID, 16)`. The fault belonged to the measurement.

      Two related cautions, kept because they are easy to get wrong again:

        * A fault address inside a DLL names a routine, not a caller. Offsets
          are not stable across compilation modes either: 0x17E580 and 0xCAB6
          were both observed in this same installed DLL, in different
          measurements. An RVA measured in one build cannot be carried into
          another build's image and treated as the same function.
        * This SDK's msctf.idl declares `ITfCompartment` as
          `SetValue(TfClientId, const VARIANT*)` + `GetValue(VARIANT*)`, which
          is not the `GetCurrentValue`/`GetCurrentValueString` pair the older
          documentation shows, and `ITfThreadMgr::GetGlobalCompartment` takes
          no GUID at all. An interop written from the older documentation
          calls the wrong vtable slots, which is a fault in the caller and
          looks exactly like a fault in the server.

      The composition failure is still real and still unexplained, and none of
      the above explains it. What is measured about it: the TIP does load in
      the target, MSCTF loads, the TSF calls return S_OK, the layout is
      0x4110411, focus is held, six of six keys reach the text service, and
      `ImmGetOpenStatus` reports the IME open - and the text still commits as
      ASCII with no preedit and no candidate window. The leading remaining
      candidate is that the session is in alphanumeric (direct input) mode
      rather than kana, which has not been read yet because the
      `TF_CONVERSIONMODE` compartment GUID is absent from this SDK's msctf.h
      and msctf.idl, and guessing a GUID is how the last three rounds of
      diagnosis went wrong.

      The line below is kept because giving an action that reads CustomActionData
      the same argument its sibling already receives is right on its own terms, and
      it is measurably harmless: the candidate built with it installs, and every
      installer test passes. It is kept **without** a claim that it repairs
      anything, because a change justified by a diagnosis that measurement then
      refuted is worse than no change - it hides the real defect.
    -->
    <SetProperty Id="EnableProfile" Value="[INSTALLFOLDER]" Before="EnableProfile" Sequence="execute" />
    <CustomAction Id="RegisterTIP" BinaryRef="Registrar" DllEntry="RegisterTIP" Execute="deferred" Impersonate="no" Return="check" />
    <CustomAction Id="RegisterRollback" BinaryRef="Registrar" DllEntry="RegisterTIPRollback" Execute="rollback" Impersonate="no" Return="check" />
    <CustomAction Id="UnregisterTIP" BinaryRef="Registrar" DllEntry="UnregisterTIP" Execute="deferred" Impersonate="no" Return="check" />
    <CustomAction Id="UnregisterRollback" BinaryRef="Registrar" DllEntry="UnregisterTIPRollback" Execute="rollback" Impersonate="no" Return="check" />
    <!--
      Impersonate="yes" is kept. It is what makes the commit run as the interactive
      user, which is the only account whose HKCU can record an input method as
      available. It was not the cause of anything measured in this area - the
      earlier belief that it was came from decoding the ActionType with the flag
      positions off by one, and that belief was wrong.
    -->
    <CustomAction Id="EnableProfile" BinaryRef="Registrar" DllEntry="EnableTipProfile" Execute="commit" Impersonate="yes" Return="check" />
    <CustomAction Id="RestoreUserIME" BinaryRef="Registrar" DllEntry="RestoreUserIMEEnvironment" Impersonate="yes" Return="ignore" />
    <CustomAction Id="ShutdownProcesses" BinaryRef="Registrar" DllEntry="ShutdownServer" Impersonate="yes" Return="ignore" />
    <!--
      Per-user input method enablement, written by the installer itself.

      Measured on the implementation host on 2026-09-28, after the AI-bundled
      MSI had been installed: the record that EnableTipProfile writes through
      InstallLayoutOrTip existed under HKU\S-1-5-18 (LOCAL SERVICE) and
      HKU\.DEFAULT, and NOT in the interactive user's own hive. The custom action
      is Execute="commit" with Impersonate="yes", the same shape upstream Mozc
      uses, but the account it ran under was not the installing user's account.
      The user who installed KanaAI therefore never had the input method enabled
      for their own session.

      This component writes the record itself, rooted at HKCU, so the client
      process writes it into the hive of the account that is installing. It is
      the difference between "the input method is installed" and "the input method
      is available to the person who installed it", which is what a one-click
      install has to deliver.

      KeyPath is on the value, so removing the component removes the record.
      The text service and profile GUIDs are the ones the product registers:
      they are TsfProfile::GetTextServiceGuid() / GetProfileGuid() as compiled
      after patch 0002, and the CLSID that the COM server writes.

      One thing that is easy to get wrong and is therefore written down. In the
      MSI Registry table, `Root` is an integer, and 1 means
      HKEY_CURRENT_USER - not HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE, which is 2. WiX 5 accepts
      Root values HKMU, HKCR, HKCU, HKLM, HKU and compiles them to -1, 0, 1, 2,
      3 in that order. This was measured rather than recalled, because getting it
      backwards would have rejected a correct fix and shipped the defect: a
      per-user MSI built with `Root="HKCU"` compiles to a row with `Root=1`, and a
      real per-user install of it logs `RegOpenKey(Root=-2147483647)` and lands
      the value under HKCU with nothing under HKLM.
    -->
    <Component Id="EnableKanaAiForCurrentUser" Guid="{6C447513-63DE-411D-8A76-E29910CD5941}" Directory="TARGETDIR">
      <RegistryKey Root="HKCU" Key="Software\Microsoft\CTF\TIP\{7E7B5C1E-6D3A-4F2C-9A0E-3F4B5D6C7E81}\LanguageProfile\0x00000411\{F3C2B7A1-6D54-4E8B-9A10-2C7D8E9F0A12}">
        <RegistryValue Name="Enable" Type="integer" Value="1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </RegistryKey>
    </Component>
    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <Custom Action="ShutdownProcesses" Before="InstallInitialize" />
      <Custom Action="RestoreUserIME" Before="ShutdownProcesses" Condition="REMOVE=&quot;ALL&quot; AND NOT UPGRADINGPRODUCTCODE" />
      <Custom Action="UnregisterRollback" Before="UnregisterTIP" Condition="REMOVE=&quot;ALL&quot;" />
      <Custom Action="UnregisterTIP" Before="RemoveFiles" Condition="REMOVE=&quot;ALL&quot;" />
      <Custom Action="RegisterRollback" Before="RegisterTIP" Condition="NOT Installed AND NOT WIX_UPGRADE_DETECTED" />
      <Custom Action="RegisterTIP" After="InstallFiles" Condition="NOT (REMOVE=&quot;ALL&quot;)" />
      <Custom Action="EnableProfile" Before="InstallFinalize" Condition="NOT (REMOVE=&quot;ALL&quot;)" />
    </InstallExecuteSequence>
  </Package>
</Wix>
